Introduction
Most discretionary reversal traders face the same problem. They see a strong move, and they know it will end somewhere. The question is where. Often they reach for a round number, a fixed pip target, or a Fibonacci level they read about years ago. The stop goes a "comfortable" distance away. These levels can work, but they are rarely checked against how the instrument in front of them has actually behaved.
The tools do not help much either. Many reversal indicators repaint, so the arrow that looked perfect in hindsight was never there in real time. Others print a confidence value, such as "82% probability", without saying how it was measured or on how many cases. A trader cannot tell whether that number comes from a thousand past events or from nothing at all. This is point A: decisions about the turning point, the stop and the target are often made on assumptions that were not tested on the market in front of us.
In this article, we build a different kind of tool. It does not replace structure-based stops, volatility-scaled targets or other valid methods. It adds one more input: levels measured on the history of the symbol we trade. We develop the Statistical Market Inflection Forecasting indicator (SMIF). It does not claim that price will reverse. It says something more modest and more useful: similar historical movements ended at these distances. They moved this far in the opposite direction and hit these levels with this frequency. Entry, stop and take profit are read from those distributions (unless an optional fallback stop is enabled). The hit rates come from a chronological replay that only uses bars that were already closed at each decision. These are historical frequencies, not guarantees. A level built from past distributions helps only if the market continues to behave similarly. We discuss cases where this assumption fails before the conclusion. When a forecast resolves, its objects leave the chart, and a single toggle brings them back for review.
System Overview
The indicator works on one analysis timeframe, which can be the chart timeframe or any timeframe from M1 to D1. It first detects confirmed reference points. A reference high is a local high confirmed by a number of bars on each side; price is then expected to move down from it. A reference low is the mirror case. After confirmation, the indicator tracks the movement that develops from the reference. When that movement has travelled a minimum distance, measured in ATR, the indicator takes a single "snapshot". At that bar it records a feature vector: distance, duration, volatility, momentum, acceleration, candle body statistics, retracement depth, the share of directional candles, time of day and the position relative to a higher-timeframe EMA.
The movement is then followed until it terminates. Termination has a formal, rule-based definition: price must retrace from the extreme by a threshold that combines ATR and a fraction of the leg. After termination, the indicator follows the opposite move until that move also terminates, or until price resumes past the original extreme. Only then does the movement become a completed historical observation. Each observation stores, in ATR units, how much further price extended after the snapshot and how far the opposite move reached. This database is the only source of statistics. Nothing about how far price "should" move is hard-coded. The rules, thresholds and percentiles, however, are parameters that we choose. They make the method repeatable, but they do not by themselves prove that it has an edge.
When a snapshot is taken, the forecasting engine selects the most similar completed observations. From the remaining-extension distribution it takes a percentile, which becomes the predicted termination and the suggested entry. The adverse excursion beyond that entry gives the stop. The opposite-move distribution gives the take profit. The engine then replays these levels on historical bars. For each neighbor, it scales the levels and counts how often price reaches the entry, stop, or target. The forecast is shown only if these empirical numbers pass the filters. Note that these counts come from the same neighbors that were used to derive the levels, so they are an in-sample measure. The forward results of forecasts tracked after their creation, shown in the validation section of the dashboard, are the more honest measure. A state machine then tracks the forecast bar by bar until it reaches TP, reaches SL, is invalidated or expires.
State
Meaning
NO_FORECAST
No developing reference and no open forecast
REFERENCE_DETECTED
A reference was confirmed on this bar
MOVEMENT_DEVELOPING
The movement from the reference is being tracked
FORECAST_READY
A forecast exists and waits for price to reach the entry (developing forecast)
ENTRY_REACHED
The entry was touched on this bar
FORECAST_ACTIVE
The entry was filled and the forecast is running (confirmed forecast)
TP_REACHED
The statistical target was reached (resolved)
SL_REACHED
The statistical stop was violated (resolved)
FORECAST_INVALIDATED
The reference broke, the target came before the entry, the entry expired, or the holding time ran out (resolved)
