The Per-Bar Pipeline
//+-------------------------------------------------------------+
//| per-bar pipeline                                            |
//+-------------------------------------------------------------+
void
ProcessBar(
const
int
i)
     {
      DetectReferences(i);
//--- pass 1: movement tracking and completion of observations
int
n=
ArraySize
(m_refs);
for
(
int
k=
0
;k<n;k++)
         TrackReference(m_refs[k],i);
//--- pass 2: snapshots and forecast evaluation (database now contains everything known at i)
for
(
int
k=
0
;k<n;k++)
if
(m_refs[k].phase==
0
&& !m_refs[k].snapshotTaken && i>=m_refs[k].confirmBar)
            TrySnapshot(m_refs[k],i);
      UpdateForecasts(i);
      CompactReferences();
      UpdateMarketInfo(i);
     }
ProcessBar is short, but its order is deliberate. First, we detect new references. Then, in a first pass, we update every tracked movement, which may complete observations and add them to the database. Only after that, in a second pass, do we take snapshots and evaluate forecasts. This means a forecast at bar i can use every observation completed at or before bar i, and nothing after. This order is valid because ProcessBar only runs on a closed bar. Every decision is taken at the close of bar i. A forecast created there can only be filled from bar i+1 onward because Simulate starts checking from the next bar. If the same logic were run inside a forming bar, it would use the high, low and close of a bar that has not finished, which is look-ahead. Open forecasts are updated next, finished references are removed, and the market information for the dashboard is refreshed.
Detecting References
//+-------------------------------------------------------------+
//|  reference detection (confirmed pivots only, never repaints)|
//+-------------------------------------------------------------+
void
DetectReferences(
const
int
i)
     {
int
lb=InpReferenceLeftBars;
int
rb=InpReferenceRightBars;
int
p =i-rb;
if
(p-lb<
0
)
return
;
bool
isHigh=
true
,isLow=
true
;
for
(
int
k=
1
;k<=lb;k++)
        {
if
(m_rates[p].high<=m_rates[p-k].high)
            isHigh=
false
;
if
(m_rates[p].low>=m_rates[p-k].low)
            isLow=
false
;
        }
for
(
int
k=
1
;k<=rb;k++)
        {
if
(m_rates[p].high<m_rates[p+k].high)
            isHigh=
false
;
if
(m_rates[p].low>m_rates[p+k].low)
            isLow=
false
;
        }
if
(isHigh)
         AddReference(REFERENCE_HIGH,p,i);
if
(isLow)
         AddReference(REFERENCE_LOW,p,i);
     }
A reference high at bar p must be strictly higher than the highs of the left bars and not lower than the highs of the right bars. The reference is only created at bar i, once all right bars are closed. This is why the detection never repaints: we are always looking back at a bar that is already fully confirmed. The strict comparison on the left and the non-strict comparison on the right also prevent two equal highs from both becoming references.
